# The typeface conformance scene (docs/styling-plan.md Slice 2b): the
# brand typeface swaps the FAMILY and nothing else.
#
# WHAT THIS SCENE EXISTS TO CATCH is the SILENT FALLBACK. Every font API
# on every platform renders SOMETHING for a family it does not have —
# Apple's Font.custom falls through to Helvetica, Pango substitutes,
# Compose's fontFamily likewise — so a typo, a stale lowering and a
# working swap are indistinguishable to every other observation kaya
# owns. `expect_typeface` reads the family the TEXT SYSTEM ended up
# with, off the real views, and that read is the whole point of the
# scene: the request echoed back would pass while nothing applied.
#
# WHY A BUNDLED FONT. The expected family has to be one string on
# every lane AND absent from every platform's defaults, and no
# installed family can be both — the GTK and Compose probes measured a
# fallback rendering IDENTICALLY to the platform default, so a scene
# asserting anything a platform ships cannot discriminate. The guests
# therefore request the VENDORED font's bytes (guests/assets/fonts/
# sora-wght.ttf, OFL, one clean family string in its name table), the
# blob registers on every backend — register-then-resolve, the same
# path a brand's licensed font takes — and "Sora" is a family no
# platform preinstalls, so a failed registration reads as the real
# fallback, never as a false pass.
#
# THE FAMILY, NEVER THE SCALE: nothing here asserts a size, and nothing
# may. Sizes, weights and metrics stay the platform's — that is the
# design (DESIGN.md), and it is also why the two Apple ramps (13pt body
# on mac, 17pt on iOS) can share one script.
#
# No opening settle: the first expect's bounded retry IS the
# scene-ready wait.
expect label#0 "typeface"
expect_typeface "Sora"
# A TYPEFACE NEVER CHANGES WHAT ANYTHING DOES. It is chrome: the field
# still takes text and the button still fires its handler. That is the
# roles' contract one tier over, and it is why the scene carries live
# widgets rather than one label.
set_text entry#0 "hi"
click button#0
expect label#1 "clicked hi"
# AND THE HEADING STILL SWAPPED, both halves of it. A text style set on
# a label OVERRIDES the root font, so a heading is exactly the label a
# root-only lowering leaves in the system face; the read above covers
# the field and the textarea, and this line keeps the heading's own AX
# contract beside it, so a lowering that traded the style for the family
# fails here.
expect_ax label#0 "heading/typeface"
